<div class="claude-inspiration theme-indigo" id="claude-inspiration">
  <div class="extras-title">🌊 A Small Note</div>
  <p>The stretch of coast between Cascais and Estoril is where Ian Fleming invented James Bond. During World War II, Estoril was genuinely the most concentrated gathering of spies in Europe — every belligerent nation used neutral Portugal as a back-channel, and the Casino Estoril, across the road from the InterContinental, was where they all relaxed, watched each other, and played cards. Fleming was stationed in Lisbon as a British naval intelligence officer and spent time at the casino. The man he watched most closely across the card table was Duško Popov — a Yugoslav playboy double agent working for both the British and the Germans, whose actual wartime story is considerably more improbable than anything Fleming ever wrote. The Bond character is a composite of several men, but Estoril is where he came into focus. You are staying at his hotel.</p>
  <p>The Souto de Moura twin towers at Casa das Histórias Paula Rego echo the conical chimneys of Sintra National Palace — Rego grew up under those chimneys, and Souto de Moura built her childhood skyline into the roofline of her museum. Stand in the museum courtyard and look up.</p>
